# Anthropic API key
# If you want to use your Claude subscription instead of an API key, see https://github.com/mattpocock/sandcastle/issues/191
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=
# GitHub personal access token — use a FINE-GRAINED PAT scoped to THIS repo only
# (contents / issues / pull-requests: read-write; workflows RW only if the
# pipeline edits .github/). Never a classic repo-scope token: the sandbox is the
# least-trusted context in the system and must not hold a credential that
# reaches any other repository.
GH_TOKEN=

# ─── Turbo remote cache ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TURBO_TOKEN and TURBO_TEAM belong in the REPO-ROOT `.env`, not in this file.
# See `.env.example` at the root.
#
# Do not add them here even "just to be safe": the sandcastle package injects
# every key PRESENT in this file into every sandbox, so a stale copy would
# shadow the values the orchestrator threads in via `docker({ env })`.
#
# To enable the Vercel remote cache (the same cache CI uses), run once in your
# terminal:
#
#   turbo login && turbo link
#
# `turbo link` writes this repo's team ID to `.turbo/config.json`, which is what
# the orchestrator's guard checks the root `.env` TURBO_TEAM against — they must
# agree or the cache is refused. Note that `turbo login` does NOT export
# anything into your shell; it only writes `~/.turbo/config.json`, so copy the
# token into the root `.env` by hand.
#
# With both set correctly, `pnpm sandcastle` prints:
#   [turbo] remote cache enabled (TURBO_TOKEN=... chars, TURBO_TEAM=... chars)
#
# If either is missing, sandcastle prints a warning and continues without the
# remote cache — sandbox runs still work, just slower (cold inputs).
